# Simulated annealing

Simulated annealing explores a problem with random proposals and sometimes
accepts a worse objective value. A temperature controls how likely those
uphill moves are. Cooling gradually makes the search more selective,
allowing early exploration to give way to local improvement. You supply
both the proposal rule and the temperature schedule.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/simulated_annealing/struct.SimulatedAnnealing.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` and a `Neighbor` proposal |
| Search | One seeded chain with uphill acceptance |
| Constraints | Unconstrained; declarations are ignored |
| Parameters | Any cloneable type, including all four dense backends |

## When to use it

Consider simulated annealing when you can define a useful random neighbor
but derivatives or conventional vector steps are unsuitable. A neighbor
can perturb continuous coordinates, swap elements of a permutation, or
modify another discrete structure. [Kirkpatrick, Gelatt, and Vecchi
(1983)](#ref-kirkpatrick-gelatt-vecchi-1983) connect this optimization
strategy to annealing in statistical mechanics.

The ability to accept uphill moves distinguishes it from strict
improvement searches such as [Solis–Wets](../../derivative-free/solis-wets/).
For continuous vectors, compare [differential
evolution](../de/) or [CMA-ES](../cma-es/) when adapting
a population is more convenient than designing a neighbor.

A useful result depends on proposals, objective scaling, temperature, and
budget. The convergence theory for slow logarithmic cooling has specific
state-space assumptions and a problem-dependent temperature coefficient
[Hajek (1988)](#ref-hajek-1988). Selecting a logarithmic schedule alone
does not give an arbitrary finite Basin run a global-optimality guarantee.

## How it works

At temperature `T`, the neighbor proposes a point `y` from the current
point `x`. Basin accepts any proposal with `f(y) <= f(x)`. A strictly
uphill proposal is accepted with probability
`exp(-(f(y) - f(x)) / T)`. This is the classical
[Metropolis rule](#ref-metropolis-1953). One iteration makes one proposal
and one objective evaluation.

The accepted point is the current chain position, and it can become worse
as the search crosses an objective barrier. Basin separately retains the
best point encountered. Rejected proposals leave the current position
unchanged and still advance the cooling schedule.

Choose a `TemperatureSchedule` explicitly. Geometric cooling uses
`T_j = T_0 * alpha^j`, reciprocal cooling uses `T_j = T_0 / (j + 1)`, and
normalized logarithmic cooling uses `T_j = T_0 * ln(2) / ln(j + 2)`.
Every schedule starts at `T_0`. With `steps_per_temperature = m`, a
temperature level lasts `m` proposals before `j` increases.

Optional reannealing resets the cooling age, bringing the next proposal
back to `T_0`. It can trigger after a fixed number of proposals, a streak
of rejections, or a period without a new best objective. If any enabled
trigger fires, all trigger counters reset together. The chain position,
historical best, and global evaluation counts remain intact.

## Example

Minimize a sphere from `[3.0, -4.0]` using symmetric uniform perturbations.
Their scale shrinks with the square root of the temperature. Each
temperature is held for eight proposals, and a fixed seed makes the run
reproducible for this deterministic objective.

<!-- compile: solver-simulated-annealing -->
```rust
use basin::core::rng::{ChaCha8Rng, RngExt};
use basin::{
    CostFunction, Executor, SimulatedAnnealing, TemperatureSchedule,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Sphere;

impl CostFunction for Sphere {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let neighbor = |x: &Vec<f64>, temperature: f64, rng: &mut ChaCha8Rng| {
        x.iter()
            .map(|v| {
                v + temperature.sqrt() * (2.0 * rng.random::<f64>() - 1.0)
            })
            .collect::<Vec<_>>()
    };
    let schedule = TemperatureSchedule::geometric(0.995)
        .with_steps_per_temperature(8);
    let solver = SimulatedAnnealing::new(neighbor, 2.0, schedule, 42);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Sphere, solver, vec![3.0, -4.0])
        .max_iter(10_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-2);
    assert!(result.best_cost() <= result.cost());
}
```

The assertion uses the best point rather than the final accepted point.
The finite iteration budget does not establish convergence to the sphere's
exact minimum.

## Practical considerations

### Design a reversible neighbor

The classical acceptance rule assumes symmetric proposals, or equal
forward and reverse proposal mass. Basin does not accept a Hastings
correction. A symmetric continuous perturbation or a uniformly chosen
swap can satisfy this contract; a directed move or a boundary repair can
change the reverse probability and needs separate justification.

Closures with signature `FnMut(&Param, F, &mut R) -> Param` implement
`Neighbor` automatically and use `Infallible` as their error type. A
fallible neighbor can implement the trait directly, with the same error
type as the objective. Proposal errors and objective errors then propagate
without erasing the application error.

Constraint declarations do not clip, project, or restrict proposals.
Encode feasible moves in the neighbor, or reject infeasible candidates
with positive infinity when that policy fits the problem. Keep the
proposal contract in mind when modifying candidates near a boundary.

### Match temperature to objective differences

The initial temperature must be finite and positive. An uphill difference
of `delta` is accepted with probability `exp(-delta / T)`, so the
appropriate scale depends on objective differences, rather than parameter
units. Multiplying the objective by a constant changes the acceptance
behavior unless the temperature scale changes with it.

The geometric factor must lie strictly between zero and one. Factors
near one and more proposals per temperature cool more slowly, spending
more evaluations before the chain becomes selective. Cooling too quickly
can leave the chain exploring only one region. Basin clamps underflowed
temperatures to the scalar's smallest positive normal value.

### Use reannealing and stopping deliberately

`with_reannealing_fixed`, `with_reannealing_accepted`, and
`with_reannealing_best` compose the three restart triggers. Despite its
name, the accepted trigger measures consecutive rejected proposals. Each
threshold must be positive. Reheating can help exploration, but it does
not independently rescale coordinates or implement adaptive simulated
annealing.

Use executor iteration, evaluation, or time limits and inspect the
termination report. The method has no ordinary temperature-based
convergence test. Rejected proposals do not trigger step- or cost-change
convergence; acceptance history remains available to executor controls
such as `no_acceptance`.

Proposed `NaN` and positive infinity are rejected. A finite proposal can
replace a current positive-infinity cost, but a current `NaN` reports
failure. Negative infinity is accepted and stops on the next framework
check. Equal finite costs are accepted.

## Implementation

`ProposalState` publishes the accepted position and matching cost, the
historical objective incumbent, evaluation counts, and acceptance history.
The solver owns the working neighbor, RNG, cooling age, and reannealing
progress. Retain it with `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect
`temperature()`, `reannealings()`, and the evolving `neighbor()`.

Fresh initialization reevaluates the start, resets cooling, and clones
the configured neighbor and RNG into fresh working components. Their
`Clone` implementations must produce independent evolving state,
including any state captured by a closure. `seed_chain(seed)` creates an
independent default-RNG chain from configured settings without consuming
the current chain's RNG.

Exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain the live components and skip
initialization. A state-only snapshot starts a fresh chain through
`Executor::new`. With `serde`, serializable neighbors and RNGs can be
included in exact checkpoints.

Parameters require only `Clone`. Supported choices include discrete
structures, `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The default `ChaCha8Rng` works on
WASM, and `new_with_rng` accepts a custom generator. Each proposal depends
on the current chain position, so the solver does not use `parallel`.

